> Terminal Piping

Piping (`|`) and redirection (`>`, `>>`, `<`) chain commands together and control input/output.

### Pipe `|`

`command1 | command2`: sends command1's output as command2's input.

example: `ls | grep "txt"` - list files, filter for "txt"
example: `cat log.txt | grep "error" | less` - search and view interactively

### Conditional Operators

`command1 && command2`: runs command2 only if command1 succeeds (exit code 0).
example: `mkdir new_folder && cd new_folder`
example: `git commit -m "msg" && git push`

`command1 || command2`: runs command2 only if command1 fails (non-zero exit).
example: `cat file.txt || echo "File not found"`
example: `ping -c 1 google.com || echo "Network unavailable"`

### Background `&`

`command &`: runs command in the background, keeping the terminal usable.
example: `firefox &`
example: `(cp -r ~/Documents/* ~/Backups/ && notify-send "done!") &`

the command is still a child of the terminal and will exit when you close it.

### Redirection

`command > file.txt`: redirects output to file (overwrites).
`command >> file.txt`: appends output to file.
`command < file.txt`: redirects file contents as input to command.

### /dev/null

`command > /dev/null`: discards command output.
`command 2> /dev/null`: discards error messages.

### tee

`command | tee file.txt`: shows output in terminal AND saves to file.
`command | tee -a file.txt`: appends to file.
